# Line endings -------------------------------------------------------------
# The `eol=lf` below is load-bearing for the red zone, and NOT redundant with
# `text=auto`: when a file has `text` set but no `eol`, `git archive` falls back
# to core.eol, which on Windows is native = CRLF. Packing on Windows would then
# emit a CRLF package and the red zone's bash would die on deploy.sh with
# `$'\r': command not found`. The target is Linux, so pin LF for everything.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary data ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The golden regression reference is a bit-exact float64 capture. `text=auto`
# already classifies it as binary (NUL byte at offset 5), but say it explicitly:
# a single EOL substitution in this file would silently corrupt the only proof
# that the legacy measurement modes still produce identical numbers.
*.npz binary

# Red-zone package contents -------------------------------------------------
# BLACKLIST, not whitelist: everything in the repo ships to the red zone unless
# listed here. New scripts are packaged automatically -- no maintenance needed.
# Only developer-facing files that are meaningless across the air gap are cut.
CLAUDE.md             export-ignore
CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT.md export-ignore
.gitignore            export-ignore
.gitattributes        export-ignore
deploy/pack.ps1       export-ignore

# VERSION stamping ----------------------------------------------------------
# `git archive` substitutes these; in a work tree they stay literal.
VERSION export-subst
